.TH IMP72C 1 "2026" "imp72"
.SH NAME
imp72c \- compile IMP72 programs to native code
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B imp72c
[\fB\-C\fR] [\fB\-S\fR | \fB\-c\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIoutput\fR] [\fB\-s\fR \fIletters\fR]
.I file.imp
\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B imp72c
compiles programs in IMP72, the extensible systems language of Ned Irons as
implemented by Walter Bilofsky for the PDP-10 (DECUS 10-198a), to native code.
The syntax of the language is read from the original syntax file
.IR syntax.imc ,
the compiler's code generator makes PDP-10 code, which is translated to an
intermediate form and from there to machine code.
.PP
IMP72 passes arguments by reference, as FORTRAN does, and a word is 64 bits.
A subroutine
.B SUBR
is therefore a C function of pointers to its arguments, returning a
.BR long ,
and a C program can call it.
.B CCALL F(A, B)
calls the C function F with the values of A and B.
Variables are common symbols.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-C
Use the C back end: the program is turned into C and compiled by the host's C
compiler.  This is the only back end on hosts other than amd64, where
the pcc code generator is used otherwise.
.TP
.B \-S
Stop after generating assembly (with \fB\-C\fR, C).
.TP
.B \-c
Stop after generating the object file.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Name of the output file.
.TP
.BI \-s " letters"
Switches for the compiler proper (listings and dumps of its tables, as in the
original: A assembly listing, T code tree, S semantics, X syntax graph).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IMP72BACKEND
.B c
or
.B pcc
to choose the back end.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/imp72/syntax.imc
The syntax of IMP72.
.TP
.I /usr/share/imp72/ffi.imc
The extension that adds CCALL.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/imp72/
The compiler proper, the back ends and the runtime library libimp72.a.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I /usr/share/doc/imp72/
.SH AUTHORS
The original IMP72 is by Walter Bilofsky; the IMP language by Ned Irons.
